   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI_05">Seefeldstrasse 54</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>parterre, rechts</addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zurich</name> VIII: <name type="geo" key="G_CHE">Switzerland</name>
                            </addrLine> </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear Miss <name type="person" key="weav76">Weaver</name>: I have received your letter and card and thank you for writing. My health is much better, I think. I hope that <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">the printing difficulties</seg> of which you speak are purely technical and that <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">the American edition</seg> is now published.</p>
                    <p>        	Allow me to wish you a happy Christmas and at the same time to thank you for your kind friendliness to me during the past year.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>        	very gratefully and sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed> <dateline>
                            <date when="1916-12-17">17/xii/916</date>
                        </dateline> </closer>
                </div> </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">In her letter to JJ of <date when="1916-11-30">30 November 1916</date>, Harriet Shaw Weaver alluded to The Egoist Ltd’s reliance on imported sheets of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name> and disclosed that it might “turn out to be impossible for the book to be ready for publication here in January” (Firth 166).</note> <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">
                    <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name> published on <date when="1916-12-29">29 December 1916</date> by the American publisher <name type="person" key="hueb76">B. W. Huebsch</name>.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
